(bright classical music)

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.710
- [Joanna] This is my four-year-old son.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.940
He's basically van Gogh.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.400
- [Son] I've got a piece
for the rainbow, mommy.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.680
- [Joanna] His latest work
might not look like much

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.360
but it's one of a kind.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.140
At least, digitally it is.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.010
Since, you know, I recently minted it

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.260
as an NFT on the Ethereum
blockchain as one does.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.830
Oh, and then I gave it to my mom

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.659
in an Ethereum wallet.

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:27.880
- What is this?

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.950
- I expect you have no idea
what any of that meant.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.419
And I didn't either until
I embarked on this project.

00:00:34.419 --> 00:00:36.514
♪ Now what the hell's an NFT? ♪

00:00:36.514 --> 00:00:38.227
- The NFTs.
- NFTs.

00:00:38.227 --> 00:00:39.170
- NFTs.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.950
- Yes, NFTs, or non-fungible tokens,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:43.730
are the thing right now.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.490
You've got artists selling
NFTs of their works,

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.600
some for millions of dollars.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.900
You've got musicians
selling NFT songs and audio.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.110
You've got brands like
Burger King, the NBA

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.240
and Macy's making NFTs.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.500
I could go on and on

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.040
but underlying that
vast and wild assortment

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.610
is the same idea.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.630
These are unique, irreplaceable

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.310
and verifiable digital things.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.200
Okay, but why would you want one?

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.033
Hmm.

00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:10.500
Authenticity, yeah.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.940
Web 3.0, which is after Web 2.0.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.010
To impress people.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.950
It's a great though tough question.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:20.370
And we're gonna get there

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.610
but first, you need to
understand what an NFT is.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.160
And the best way to do that,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.180
make or buy one yourself.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.797
Okay, so this is my son's
art or what he calls.

00:01:30.797 --> 00:01:34.760
- A masterpiece rainbow unicorn.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.720
- You wanna call her NFT?

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.270
- No.
- No?

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.750
- Boring.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.860
- He made it with physical stuff

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.880
and then I quickly turned it digital.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.300
I scanned it with my iPhone

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.747
and asked a colleague to
animate some of the parts.

00:01:47.747 --> 00:01:48.580
(horse neighing)

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.420
I ended up with this basic MP4 video file.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.640
Sure, I could just upload it to the web

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.650
or some social media platform.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:56.950
But there, nobody knows

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.490
that this is the real original thing.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.950
That it's not just some digital copy.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.730
There's nothing, no digital info

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:07.750
that says this is the
masterpiece unicorn rainbow NFT

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:09.300
made by my son.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.160
That's what minting it into an NFT does.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.420
Yep, minting, which is the first piece

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.480
of jargon you need to understand.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:17.450
Not these mints.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.190
Minting an NFT is the process

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:22.510
of taking a piece of
content, a JPEG, an MP4,

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.750
an MP3, some other file

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.520
and putting it on a digital registry.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:28.540
In this process,

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.693
you're recording the stamp
of ownership on a blockchain.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.740
Jargon term number two, blockchain.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.720
Now, this could be its own vide

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.610
but here's the simple explanation.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.630
The blockchain is a software network

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:43.600
where computers run separate copies

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.510
of the same program.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.830
The computers are linked

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.320
but no one controls the network.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.660
It's called a decentralized network,

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.810
and it processes transactions.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.840
It also keeps a ledger or database

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.030
with records of every single
transaction that happens,

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.660
every transfer, every purchase, et cetera.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.140
There are many blockchains,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.830
including the one for Bitcoin,

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.960
the crypto you've
probably heard all about.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.270
Got all that?

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.690
All right, back to NFT making.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.020
I decided to mint the
great rainbow unicorn

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:15.940
using a service called XIP.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.380
But to get started on XIP,

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.310
I needed to make a crypto wallet.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:20.830
Think of like a real wallet

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.890
but only for the cryptocurrencies

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.450
and NFTs that you own for
a specific blockchain.

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:27.283
For this project,

00:03:27.283 --> 00:03:30.490
I went with the gold
standard for NFTs, Eth.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:32.380
It's the cryptocurrency associated

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.520
with the Ethereum blockchain.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.780
MetaMask is one of the
most popular Eth wallets.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.430
Even though I was making an NFT,

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.440
I needed Ethereum for
various associated fees,

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:43.860
which I'll get to.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.500
So I bought some on Coinbase,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.940
a crypto exchange where
you can buy and sell lots

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.420
of different currencies,

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:53.120
and then transferred that to
my MetaMask Ethereum wallet.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.620
Finally, I was ready.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.160
I gave the NFT a title and description

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:58.920
and then uploaded it to mint.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.210
But before it was official,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.650
I had to pay what's called a gas fee.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:05.060
For this one, I went
straight to Jeff Cluck,

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.930
the co-founder and CEO of XIP.

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:09.553
- On the Ethereum blockchain,

00:04:09.553 --> 00:04:13.200
gas refers to the cost necessary

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.500
to conduct a transaction

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.210
on the Ethereum network.

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:18.953
It's the cost associated

00:04:18.953 --> 00:04:21.370
with powering the network.

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:24.850
There are fees associated
with other blockchain networks

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.990
but typically, Ethereum is known

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:30.057
to have some of the highest gas fees.

00:04:30.057 --> 00:04:34.350
- So I paid the gas fee, .0333
ETH or $145.18, and boom.

00:04:39.123 --> 00:04:41.430
(screen twinkling)

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.870
It's on the blockchain.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:43.930
I could even confirm it

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:46.900
by going to Etherscan.io,
which has a record

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:49.130
of every transaction on the blockchain.

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:51.330
Okay, so now I could do a few things

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:54.780
with this NFT masterpiece,
including sell it.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.420
And oh boy, are there a lot

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:59.440
of digital art NFTs for sale out there.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.730
OpenSea, the biggest NFT marketplace

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:03.710
has millions of pieces.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:06.910
And some of them have
crazy high valuations.

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:10.260
Look at this Cool Cat #6133.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.370
The highest offer was $26,000
as of making this video.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:15.780
Why so much?

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.850
- Some of these new NFT projects,

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:22.080
such as the Cool Cats, these
are some of the most in-demand,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.168
most exciting NFT projects

00:05:24.168 --> 00:05:26.200
that are out there right now

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.340
because of the way that
they engage a community

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:30.890
around their project.

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:34.840
They have in real life meetups,
events, people connect.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.370
- I didn't sell or list the
masterpiece unicorn rainbow

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.530
because I'm not in the art business

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:40.970
but selling is pretty easy

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:42.150
once you get to this point.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:44.440
You go to OpenSea, set a minimum price

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.030
and see what happens.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:47.180
Just like eBay.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.310
Buying an NFT doesn't take as many steps

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:50.930
as making or selling one

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:52.700
but most sites, like OpenSea,

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:54.970
require you have a crypto wallet.

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:56.910
But remember, you're gonna pay a gas fee

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.720
on top of the price of the art,

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.260
which makes this all very expensive.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.110
Instead of selling,

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:04.865
I decided to gift my NFT to my mom.

00:06:04.865 --> 00:06:07.210
Daughter of the year right here.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:10.900
Her very own MetaMask Ethereum wallet.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.070
Yep, in that envelope,

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:14.350
I put her new MetaMask wallet information,

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:16.280
which contains her new NFT.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:18.360
- What in the world?

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.480
- This is your crypto wallet,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:24.860
which inside, it has an
NFT made by your grandson.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.850
- Why would I want this?

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:29.590
- And that's really the
question of the year.

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:32.440
Why would you want an NFT?

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.166
After speaking with lots
of people for this story,

00:06:35.166 --> 00:06:37.350
I came up with a few reasons.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:40.000
One, this is an elaborate, high-tech,

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.300
energy-sapping way to make money

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:46.600
because well, people and
companies like money.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:48.910
Then there's the idea
of owning digital assets

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:50.000
in the metaverse.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:51.600
That is the coming virtual world

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.870
where we work, and shop, and hang out.

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:55.980
Then there's that Cool Cats idea.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:59.230
You buy an NFT to gain
access to a community.

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:02.440
Another popular one is called
the Bored Ape Yacht Club

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.760
with members like Steph
Curry and Jimmy Fallon.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.150
And then finally, you
love an artist or brand

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:10.870
and you wanna own
something unique from them,

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:14.260
which I can confidently
say is exactly how I feel

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:16.988
about my favorite NFT artist.

00:07:16.988 --> 00:07:18.735
NFT.

00:07:18.735 --> 00:07:19.568
- NFPP.

00:07:21.903 --> 00:07:22.736
NFT.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:26.200
- [Joanna] What does it mean?

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:27.033
- Boop.

